//! The name, MIME type and conventional extension of every format.
//!
//! Generated by `scripts/gen_kinds.ps1` from the format tables in
//! `readme.md` plus the metadata table at the top of that script. Do not
//! edit by hand; `tests/kinds_meta.rs` holds it against `SIGNATURE_KIND`.
//!
//! The readme is the source for the display names because it is what a reader
//! sees, and a crate whose `Debug` output says `Png` where its documentation
//! says "PNG" has two names for one thing. The MIME types and extensions are
//! in the generator because they are not in the readme and are not derivable
//! from magic bytes: `50 4B 03 04` is a zip container, and whether that is
//! `application/zip` or `application/java-archive` or
//! `application/vnd.android.package-archive` is a question about the bytes
//! *inside* it, which is a longer answer than a magic number.

use crate::magical::magic::FileKind;

/// Every format, in the order the metadata table is sorted.
///
/// Sorted by the short name the Python binding uses as its enum value -- `7z`
/// before `ace` before `amr` -- and by nothing else. It is deliberately not
/// `SIGNATURE_KIND` order, which is the order detection has to try rules in:
/// 71 of its 114 entries are out of enum order, so a catalogue built from it
/// reads as though something were wrong with the formats near the end. Nor is
/// it enum order, which is neither alphabetical nor grouped, and which changed
/// meaning last time a format was added in the section it belonged to.
///
/// A display name, a MIME type or an extension is the stable way to name a
/// format in a table, and a list of those is what a reader can scan.
